  "text": "Solution 1 is the only one with a checkable mechanism. Solutions 2 and 3 rest on heuristic thresholds (\"$(d-1)/4 = 1$\" and an unspecified \"noise cascade\") that aren't derived from the averaging dynamics. Solution 1 sets up an explicit two-lineage distance process and tests for a discrete eigenvalue below the tree's continuous-spectrum edge. Its algebra checks out: the discriminant of $u^2+(3-d)u+d$ is $(d-1)(d-9)$, so the quadratic has real roots only for $d\\ge 9$. At $d=9$, $s_1=3<9-4\\sqrt2\\approx 3.343$, and for $d\\le 8$ there is no bound state.\n\nI could not verify the transition rates in Solution 1's distance process from first principles, so my confidence is moderate, not high. The answer $d_c=8$ is the most plausible of the three, but it is not established.\n\nCHOICE: 1",
